From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm: airoha: an7583: probe the real DRAM size

Same problem and same fix as 206 does for an7581: dram_init() took the size
straight from the device tree, and fdt_fixup_memory_banks() then pushed that
number into the kernel's dtb, so a board fitted with a larger part than the
dts admits to ran with the excess unreachable.

The two SoCs' init.c are line-for-line identical apart from the name in
print_cpuinfo() and the mem_map variable, and the probe itself is pure
address aliasing -- it writes an anchor low in DRAM, writes again one
candidate size higher, and sees whether the second write wrapped onto the
first.  It touches no SoC register and no controller state, so it carries
over unchanged.

The anchor sits above the ATF reservation at the bottom of DRAM, and both
words are restored before returning, so this runs safely before relocation.

Every step is printed.  The board that ends up with a surprising size is
exactly the board whose owner has no serial console attached, and dram_init()
runs early enough that these lines land in the console recording the recovery
page serves at /log -- so the derivation, not just the answer, is there to
read afterwards.  A 1 GiB part carrying the 512 MiB dts now says

  dram: probing by address aliasing, base 0x80000000
  dram:  anchor at 0x80200000, holds 0x00000000
  dram:   512 MiB: wrote 0xa0200000, anchor now 0x5a5aa5a5 -- no wrap, part is larger
  dram:  1024 MiB: wrote 0xc0200000, anchor now 0xa5a55a5a -- wrapped onto the anchor
  dram: 1024 MiB probed, device tree says 512 MiB
  dram: going with the probe; this is also what fdt_fixup_memory_banks() hands the kernel

Writing it out also exposed a hole worth closing: the loop starts at 512 MiB,
so on a smaller part every candidate wraps and the probe answers 512 MiB --
U-Boot would then hand the kernel memory that is not there.  Nothing below
the floor can be measured this way, so a dts below it is now taken as-is and
the probe skipped.

Not verified on hardware: no AN7583 board on hand.  On an7581 the same code
was checked against mmap-aliased memory for the 512 MiB, 1 GiB and 2 GiB
cases; nothing in it is SoC-specific, but a first boot on real hardware
should still confirm the reported size.

--- a/arch/arm/mach-airoha/an7583/init.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-airoha/an7583/init.c
@@ -16,9 +16,99 @@ int print_cpuinfo(void)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The device tree carries a conservative size, because one image ships to
+ * boards fitted with different parts and nothing in the boot chain reports
+ * the real one -- BL2 configures the controller and moves on.  Find it by
+ * address aliasing instead: on a part smaller than the address being probed,
+ * a write high up wraps around and lands back on the low anchor.
+ *
+ * The anchor sits above the ATF reservation at the bottom of DRAM, and both
+ * words are put back before returning, so this is safe to run this early.
+ *
+ * Every step is printed.  A board that ends up with the wrong size is
+ * exactly the board whose owner has no serial console attached, and these
+ * lines are early enough to survive into the console recording that the
+ * recovery page serves at /log -- so the derivation, not just the answer,
+ * is there to read afterwards.
+ */
+static ulong an7583_probe_ram_size(ulong base)
+{
+	volatile u32 *lo = (volatile u32 *)(base + SZ_2M);
+	u32 save_lo = *lo;
+	ulong size;
+
+	printf("dram: probing by address aliasing, base 0x%lx\n", base);
+	printf("dram:  anchor at 0x%lx, holds 0x%08x\n", (ulong)lo, save_lo);
+
+	for (size = SZ_512M; size < SZ_2G; size <<= 1) {
+		volatile u32 *hi = (volatile u32 *)((ulong)lo + size);
+		u32 save_hi = *hi;
+		u32 got;
+
+		*lo = 0x5a5aa5a5;
+		*hi = 0xa5a55a5a;
+		__asm__ __volatile__("dsb sy" : : : "memory");
+		got = *lo;
+
+		printf("dram:  %4lu MiB: wrote 0x%lx, anchor now 0x%08x -- %s\n",
+		       size >> 20, (ulong)hi, got,
+		       got == 0x5a5aa5a5 ? "no wrap, part is larger" :
+					   "wrapped onto the anchor");
+
+		if (got != 0x5a5aa5a5) {
+			/* Wrapped, so *hi and *lo are the same word. */
+			*hi = save_hi;
+			*lo = save_lo;
+
+			return size;
+		}
+
+		*hi = save_hi;
+	}
+
+	*lo = save_lo;
+	printf("dram:  nothing wrapped below 2048 MiB\n");
+
+	return SZ_2G;
+}
+
 int dram_init(void)
 {
-	return fdtdec_setup_mem_size_base();
+	ulong dts, probed;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = fdtdec_setup_mem_size_base();
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	dts = gd->ram_size;
+
+	/*
+	 * The loop starts at 512 MiB, so on a smaller part every candidate
+	 * wraps and the answer would be 512 MiB -- U-Boot would then hand the
+	 * kernel memory that is not there.  Nothing below the floor can be
+	 * measured this way, so below it the device tree is the better source.
+	 */
+	if (dts && dts < SZ_512M) {
+		printf("dram: %lu MiB from the device tree; below the 512 MiB probe floor, not probing\n",
+		       dts >> 20);
+
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	probed = an7583_probe_ram_size(gd->ram_base);
+	gd->ram_size = probed;
+
+	if (probed == dts) {
+		printf("dram: %lu MiB, agrees with the device tree\n", probed >> 20);
+	} else {
+		printf("dram: %lu MiB probed, device tree says %lu MiB\n",
+		       probed >> 20, dts >> 20);
+		printf("dram: going with the probe; this is also what fdt_fixup_memory_banks() hands the kernel\n");
+	}
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 int dram_init_banksize(void)
